  /* ══ THE MIT ART — the PlayStation pads are somebody else's drawing ══
   *
   * The owner asked three times for the drawing from
   * github.com/dualshock-tools/dualshock-tools.github.io, so the PlayStation
   * preview IS that art now: MIT licensed, © 2024 the_al, full permission
   * notice at the top of this file and in ZSART.credits() so the app can
   * print it too. The rest of this file's one-original-silhouette rule is
   * unchanged — it governs pad(), padSmall() and button(), which are ours.
   *
   * PATH DATA IS COPIED BYTE FOR BYTE. Almost every command in it is
   * RELATIVE, so re-rounding one coordinate moves every point after it and
   * the silhouette drifts. Nothing here is prettified.
   *
   * WHAT WAS STRIPPED FROM THEIR FILES, AND WHY
   *   style="width:80%;height:auto;max-width:80%" on the root — it pinned the
   *     pad to 80% of whatever box it landed in, so it could never fill our
   *     card. preview2d sizes its own root.
   *   transform="translate(0,0.65) scale(0.70)" — it was repeated on EVERY
   *     group in the DualSense file. It is folded into the one matrix below.
   *   duplicate ids (<g id="Mute_infill"><path id="Mute_infill">, and
   *     L3_outline twice in the DualShock) — ids are document-global, two
   *     pads on one page would collide, and the audit below fails a
   *     duplicate. Nothing here emits a source id at all.
   *   the L2/R2 "0 %" <text> readouts and their #1a237e — we have a gauge,
   *     and that navy is from their light theme.
   *   every fill/stroke — there were none to strip. Their file declares no
   *     paint; their JS sets it at runtime (light blue shell, white infills).
   *     Ours is set below, dark-theme first.
   *
   * HOW THEIR PARTS BECAME OUR CONTRACT — this mapping is the whole job,
   * because a name that does not match is a button that never lights:
   *
   *   Cross_infill                → south      Triangle_infill → north
   *   Circle_infill               → east       Square_infill   → west
   *   Up/Down/Left/Right_infill   → dpad_up/dpad_down/dpad_left/dpad_right
   *   L1_infill / R1_infill       → l1 / r1
   *   Create_infill               → select     Options_infill  → start
   *   PS_infill                   → guide      Trackpad_infill → touchpad
   *   L3_surround (DS5)           → the stick WELL, static art
   *   L3_outline  (DS4)           → the same, that file's only stick ring
   *   L2_infill / R2_infill       → the CLIP the pull gauge floods
   *
   *   Mute_infill (DS5) is DECOR, not a hotspot: the board has no code for
   *   the mute key, and a hotspot with no binding behind it is a button that
   *   does nothing when you tap it. Same rule the Xbox share button follows.
   *   Speaker_grill, every *_outline ring, and the DualShock's nine body
   *   seams are decor too — they carry the shape detail livePad never
   *   touches, which is exactly where shape detail has to live.
   *
   * WHAT HAS NO SOURCE ART: Xbox. There is no Xbox drawing in that
   * repository, and a PlayStation pad shown to an Xbox player is worse than
   * no picture. 'xb' (and 'nin' and 'generic', which fell through to it
   * before and still do) keep xb2d below, unchanged.
   */

  /* Generated from their two SVGs; see docs/DESIGN.md and the notice above.
     box = the art's own content bounds, so the frame is tight rather than
     the 640×518 their page happened to use. ls/rs/lt/rt/guide/face are
     anchors in THAT space — every number our side needs is derived from
     them at draw time, so there are no magic pixel constants below. */
  var MITART = {
    ds5: {
      box: [2.08, 1.6, 909.73, 731.12],
      well: 64.64,
